  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "EhYaGEIuGLTXBcgKJgqEBipYDZQ=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:38:20.954Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "A processor is supposed to run a strictly one-way data flow: operations arrive through `onOperations`, and any resulting mutation leaves through `dispatch`. The `dispatch` object is handed to the factory on `IProcessorHostModule`, and its only method is `execute`:\n\n```typescript\ninterface IProcessorDispatch {\n  execute(\n    docId: string,\n    branch: string,\n    actions: Action[],\n    signal?: AbortSignal,\n    meta?: Record<string, unknown>,\n  ): Promise<ProcessorDispatchResult>;\n}\n\ntype ProcessorDispatchResult = {\n  id: string;      // job ID\n  status: string;  // job status at time of submission\n};\n```\n\nUnder the hood `dispatch.execute()` calls `reactorClient.executeAsync()`. The returned `status` is the status *at submission*, so awaiting the promise tells you the job was accepted, never that it landed. The canonical shape is a processor that watches for a state transition and fires an action at a different document:\n\n```typescript\nif (operation.action.type === \"SET_STATUS\" &&\n    operation.action.input?.status === \"approved\") {\n  await this.dispatch.execute(this.paymentDocId, \"main\", [\n    { type: \"CREATE_PAYMENT\",\n      input: { invoiceId: context.documentId, amount: operation.action.input.amount },\n      scope: \"global\" },\n  ]);\n}\n```\n\nThe processor dispatches and moves on. If it needs to observe the result, the correct move is to widen its own `ProcessorFilter` to include the target document type so the resulting operations come back through a later `onOperations` call \u2014 not to await the outcome. That constraint is what keeps a processor from waiting on work that depends on the processor itself finishing.\n\nBecause backfill and restart-gap replay can re-deliver the same operations, a dispatching processor also wants an idempotence guard: check the target document's state (or a dedup key in it) before dispatching, so a replayed batch does not double-fire the side action.\n\nProvenance: academy `03-Build/04-WorkWithData/05-ProcessorBestPractices.md` (published), confirmed against `packages/shared/processors/types.ts` and `apps/connect/src/store/processor-host-module.ts` at monorepo 6.2.2-dev.45.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-13T14:38:20.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:38:20.954Z"
    }
  },
